      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "An inspector may enter a place, including a conveyance, to verify compliance or prevent non-compliance with the Act or regulations, and may open and examine receptacles and packages, examine things used or capable of being used to produce, preserve, package or store controlled substances or precursors, examine and copy labels, advertising, records and electronic data (other than medical records of persons), photograph and record, take samples for analysis, seize and detain substances, precursors, designated devices or conveyances, order things or conveyances to be moved or not moved, order people to establish their identity, and order a person to stop or start an activity covered by the Act. Entry is limited to places where the inspector reasonably believes a controlled substance, precursor, designated device or administration-related document is located, or an activity could be conducted under a licence, permit, authorization or exemption the Minister is considering, among the other cases listed. An inspector may enter a dwelling-house only with an occupant’s consent or under a justice’s warrant, and may not use force unless accompanied by a peace officer and the warrant specifically authorizes force. Anyone at the place must give the inspector reasonable assistance and information. Seized items not returned or dealt with within 120 days are dealt with under the regulations or as the Minister directs.",
